#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049363 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048272 :topic/math-academy-id 428 :topic/title "Factoring Cubic Expressions by Grouping"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049153 :topic/math-academy-id 441 :topic/title "Factoring Sums and Differences of Cubes"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049832 :topic/math-academy-id 1422 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations with Leading Coefficients by Factoring"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050323 :topic/math-academy-id 1676 :topic/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using Polynomial Factorization"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334 :topic/math-academy-id 425 :topic/title "The Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation"}] :topic/id #uuid "eefd5285-6b99-569d-a5bb-365f176fa757" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087543 :question/id #uuid "e429a65a-d65d-5b3f-a054-e3ab8a61caf3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5299" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087545 :question/id #uuid "011cf746-baa5-5395-8515-3beb98ff22a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5302" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-6x+8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x = 4.$ Therefore, $x = 4$ is the only vertical asymptote."} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087544 :question/id #uuid "f4879578-ac07-54f8-b00f-5ab651ae240a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5301" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x^3+x^2+4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x({x}^{2}+x+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0.$\n\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(1{)}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =1-16 \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087546 :question/id #uuid "51ff19a3-5c58-5c26-9c29-14ffc7d284db" :question/math-academy-id "e-5304" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x^3 - 3 x^2 + x - 3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{({x}^{2}+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(0{)}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :topic/math-academy-id 807 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048152 :topic/math-academy-id 3355 :topic/title "Solving Rational Inequalities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048469 :topic/math-academy-id 1817 :topic/title "Locating Holes in Rational Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049128 :topic/math-academy-id 738 :topic/title "Identifying a Rational Function From a Graph"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049886 :topic/math-academy-id 2189 :topic/title "Infinite Limits of Rational Functions"}] :topic/title "Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048245 :topic/math-academy-id 3770 :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486 :topic/math-academy-id 1181 :topic/title "Verifying Solutions of Differential Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489 :topic/math-academy-id 478 :topic/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :topic/id #uuid "f00b1f48-2a5d-5edc-ba37-2d8457af2735" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096496 :question/id #uuid "e8b9014e-32cf-5f04-856c-7fd663bea50f" :question/math-academy-id "e-528" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f'(x) - 3\\sin(2x) = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3\\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Differential Equation Using Integration"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096497 :question/id #uuid "a8e6c58c-da62-5dab-8325-a53c14d9b8ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-529" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+Cx+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$ We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not."} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096498 :question/id #uuid "ab40a4d3-c418-5b64-a04c-bb017155a4d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-530" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $y'  = 2x,$ and sketch the solution curves of this equation." :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^2+C.\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,1.$\n\n![](images/e3/e37771799ef5fadc259e6ea0073042a83e0974b5a2619d1af3e87a7467e6d780.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Solution Curves of a Differential Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096699 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096499 :question/id #uuid "29a0265e-ad88-5ac9-88fe-878aa4f0d0bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-531" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0)=2.$\n\n$$\nf'(x) - \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2} = 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x) = 3\\arctan{x} + C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0) = 2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ 2 & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\arctan x + 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of a Differential Equation With a Given Condition"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1061 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569 :topic/math-academy-id 877 :topic/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697 :topic/math-academy-id 466 :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Separation of Variables"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049617 :topic/math-academy-id 3717 :topic/title "Stream Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869 :topic/math-academy-id 1951 :topic/title "Calculating Potential Functions"}] :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Direct Integration"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049406 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127 :topic/math-academy-id 876 :topic/title "Exponential Growth and Decay Models With First-Order ODEs"}] :topic/id #uuid "f1df9c16-7f76-55a1-b26f-68e71c4a5125" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124373 :question/id #uuid "d178c83a-b8af-5d6a-bf40-fcfb7506feeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6686" :question/problem "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4(60-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and $a=60.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y \\lt 60$ for all $t \\ge 0.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124370 :question/id #uuid "a568fc63-e81c-5210-93f0-bcbd429a9e40" :question/math-academy-id "e-6678" :question/problem "A biologist observed that the growth of the number of fish in a lake is directly proportional to the difference between the lake’s maximum fish population and the current population. Let $P(t)$ be the fish population, measured in number of fish, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum fish population that the lake can support is $200$ fish, which differential equation could be used to model the lake’s fish population? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the fish population $P(t)$ of the lake changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum fish population the lake can support and the current fish population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(a - P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum fish population the lake can support, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 200 \\textrm{ fish},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(200 - P).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124371 :question/id #uuid "d603efdd-6e27-5e14-a106-f9fc4a182403" :question/math-academy-id "e-6679" :question/problem "In an aquarium, there are initially $20$ fish. After $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ Assuming inhibited growth, calculate the number of fish after $100$ weeks if the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $90$ fish." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of fish $N(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total number of fish and the current number of fish. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k\\left(a-N\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 90$ fish, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(90-N).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dN}{90-N} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dN}{90-N} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90-N| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $90 - N > 0$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $N(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90-N & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 90-N & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 90-N & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ N & =90-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial number of fish was $20.$ So, we apply the initial condition $N(0)=20,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =20 \\\\ 90-K{e}^{0} & =20 \\\\ 90-K & =20 \\\\ K & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $N(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nN(t)=90 - 70 e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ So, applying the condition $N(30)=50,$ we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(30) & =50 \\\\ 90-70{e}^{-30k} & =50 \\\\ 40 & =70{e}^{-30k} \\\\ {e}^{-30k} & =\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ -30k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nN(t)=90-70e^{(t/30)\\,\\ln\\left(4/7\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally,  the number of fish after $t=100$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(100) & =90-70{e}^{(100/30)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4/7)} \\\\  & =79.161… \\\\  & ≈79\\,\\text{fish},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1924 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049131 :topic/math-academy-id 649 :topic/title "Inhibited Decay Models With First-Order ODEs"}] :topic/title "Inhibited Growth Models With First-Order ODEs"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049456 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048432 :topic/math-academy-id 853 :topic/title "Introduction to Ellipses"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048811 :topic/math-academy-id 842 :topic/title "Equations of Circles Centered at the Origin"}] :topic/id #uuid "f6e279fc-81eb-5f98-9ec1-265f25361906" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087839 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087515 :question/id #uuid "446ea007-1ff4-5023-8e3f-7f00eb0fb2d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5071" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d2ba3fba1a05a841a486553ea3a52d6f3797974630420ad79ae50f4c6f5bc15.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087516 :question/id #uuid "af56117c-65e0-5f12-9780-7a95e6be829e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5072" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](images/3e/3e8d5ed4bf055960b50e046c0688a3c4cc69907ebb1209bb9658ac4bcba48ffe.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087517 :question/id #uuid "b7c94673-f847-5977-9b9f-8d235ea42eb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5073" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{36} + \\dfrac{y^2}{25} = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $a^2=36$ and $b^2=25.$\n\n- Since $a^2=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36} = 6.$\n\n- Since $b^2=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25} = 5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a = 2 \\cdot 6 = 12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b = 2 \\cdot 5 =10.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :topic/math-academy-id 848 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048894 :topic/math-academy-id 1824 :topic/title "Orthogonal Trajectories"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699 :topic/math-academy-id 849 :topic/title "Equations of Ellipses Centered at a General Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051063 :topic/math-academy-id 4392 :topic/title "Describing Planar Regions Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :topic/title "Equations of Ellipses Centered at the Origin"}]}
